'd be left with T7, T7, B6, B6\\n- I could win by drawing the last T7 (forming T7,T7,T7 pung + B6,B6 pair)\\n- Or by drawing T5 or T8 to form T6,T7,T8 chow with one T7, leaving T7,T7,T7 and B6,B6\\n\\n**If I play B6:**\\n- I'd be left with T7, T7, T7, B6\\n- I could win by drawing B6 (but 0 B6s remain - impossible!)\\n- Or by drawing T5, T6, or T8 to complete a chow with T7s\\n\\nGiven that there are NO B6s left unshown, keeping both B6s is pointless for forming a pair to win. The last T7 is still available, and there are 3 T6s remaining which could help form T6,T7,T8.\\n\\nReason:\\nPlaying B6 is the better choice because:\\n1. There are 0 B6s left in unshown tiles, so the pair cannot help me win\\n2. Keeping three T7s gives me flexibility - I can form a Pung with them OR use them in a chow with T6 (3 remaining) or T5/T8\\n3. This maximizes my winning opportunities with the remaining tile pool\\n\\nAnswer: Play B6\"}"}
